# The wireless regulatory database, put into the INITRAMFS rather than left on
# the rootfs where wireless-regdb installs it.
#
# WHY IT HAS TO BE HERE. cfg80211 is built into this kernel (CONFIG_CFG80211=y)
# and asks the firmware loader for regulatory.db during its own init. Measured
# on the device:
#
#     [1.622] faux_driver regulatory: Direct firmware load for regulatory.db
#             failed with error -2
#     [1.625] Freeing unused kernel memory
#     [7.141] EXT4-fs (loop0p2): mounted filesystem ... r/w
#
# The request happens five and a half seconds before the real rootfs exists, so
# it can only ever be satisfied from the initramfs. cfg80211 does not retry and
# cannot be reloaded, so a copy that arrives later is never seen - installing
# wireless-regdb alone changes nothing, and neither does a reboot.
#
# WHAT IT UNLOCKS. Without the database the kernel falls back to its built-in
# world domain, which marks every 5 GHz rule PASSIVE-SCAN and refuses `iw reg
# set` outright - the domain stays "country 00" no matter what is asked for.
# That is what limits this device on 5 GHz, and it is a software gap rather
# than anything about the radio, which advertises Band 2 with 68 frequencies.
/lib/firmware/regulatory.db
/lib/firmware/regulatory.db.p7s
